! Buchholz (ed.), "Planning a Computer System" (1962), APPENDIX B,
! B.6 "Transposition of a Large Bit Matrix (Table B.6)", table and
! notes printed p.303 (PDF 328), text printed p.304 (PDF 329), read
! from the page images.  Notation: pp.292-294.
!
!   "A square 64n-by-64n bit matrix beginning at address A.0 is to be
!   transposed and stored at nonoverlapping addresses starting at B.0.
!   The technique chosen here is to assemble 64 successive bits of a
!   column into a 64-bit word in the accumulator and then store that
!   word in a row of the transposed matrix."  The example "illustrates
!   the use of bit address and offset indexing with VFL operations".
!
! TABLE B.6 AS PRINTED, with the STRAP statement beside each line:
!
!   100.0   LOAD INDEX, X1, 201.0                    Start  LX, $1, 201.0
!   100.32  LOAD INDEX, X2, 202.0                           LX, $2, 202.0
!   101.0   LOAD INDEX, X3, 203.0                           LX, $3, 203.0
!   101.32  CONNECT 0011 (V + I) (VBU, 1, 1), N.0 (X1), 63 (X3)
!                                    (1)   C0011(V+I)(BU,1,1), 2.0($1), 63($3)
!   102.32  SUBTRACT IMMEDIATE FROM VALUE COUNT AND REFILL, X3, 0.32
!                                    (2)   V-ICR, $3, 0.32
!   103.0   BRANCH XCZ (F), 101.32   (3)   BZXCZ, 101.32
!   103.32  STORE (V + IC) (VBU, 64, 8), 1.0 (X2)
!                                    (4)   ST(V+IC)(BU,64,8), 1.0($2)
!   104.32  BRANCH XCZ (F), 101.32   (5)   BZXCZ, 101.32
!   105.0   LOAD COUNT IMMEDIATE, X2, N        (6)   LCI, $2, 2
!   105.32  ADD TO VALUE AND COUNT, X1, 204.0  (7)   V+C, $1, 204.0
!   106.0   BRANCH XCZ (F), 101.32                   BZXCZ, 101.32
!   106.32  BRANCH ENABLED AND WAIT, 106.32    Stop  BEW, 106.32
!
!   201.0   INDEX, A.0, 64*N, 201.0            XW, 1000.0, 128, 201.0
!   202.0   INDEX, B.0, N, 202.0               XW, 2000.0, 2, 202.0
!   203.0   INDEX, 0.0, 64, 203.0              XW, 0.0, 64, 203.0
!   204.0   VALUE, -N*N*64.0 + 0.01            VF, -255.63
!
!   Notes: (1) Assemble 64 successive column bits in the right half
!         accumulator by indexing the offset from 63 to 0.
!     (2) 0.32 means a 1 in the low-order bit of the 19-bit address in
!         this instruction, which matches the low-order position of the
!         offset field; thus the effective offset will be reduced by 1.
!     (3) Branch if the index count is not zero.
!     (4) Store 64 row bits of the transposed matrix.
!     (5) Traverse the loop n times.
!     (6) Reset x2 count to n.
!     (7) Advance the column index to the start of the next column by
!         subtracting the length of the column (64n**2 words) and
!         adding 0.01.
!
! THE MAPPINGS, by page of the book's Appendix A.4:
!   CONNECT (C) p.281, connective 0011 = the memory bit (p.281-282).
!   Progressive indexing p.280, with the codes of printed p.294:
!   "(V + I) add immediate to value", "(V + IC) (same) and count".
!   SUBTRACT IMMEDIATE FROM VALUE, COUNT, AND REFILL (V - ICR), ADD TO
!   VALUE AND COUNT (V + C) p.283; LOAD COUNT IMMEDIATE (LCI) p.282;
!   STORE (ST) p.277.  BRANCH XCZ (F) is BRANCH ON INDICATOR, index
!   count zero, "branch if indicator off" (p.284, p.293): STRAP's
!   BZXCZ.
!   "63 (X3)" is an indexed OFFSET: the VFL format is
!   "O(M, L, BS), A(I), F(I) ... there may be one [I] for modifying the
!   address and another for modifying the offset" (printed p.293).
!   Note (2)'s arithmetic is Reference Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.21
!   (PDF 24): "In the variable field instructions, the 19 bits which
!   form the length, byte size, and offset field are indexed as a
!   single field" -- so the half-word bit of x3's value, 0.32, lines
!   up with the low-order offset bit.
!   -N*N*64.0 + 0.01 with n = 2 is -256.0 + 0.01 = -255.63 (63 bits
!   short of 256 words; carries past the period are modulo 64, p.293).
!
! >>> THE TABLE AS PRINTED DOES NOT TRANSPOSE THE MATRIX, AND THE
! >>> FAULT IS THE BOOK'S: THE BYTE SIZE IN LINE 101.32.
!
! "(VBU, 1, 1)" is a one-bit field with byte size 1.  The book's own
! section 7.14 (printed pp.90-91, PDF 115-116) says: "When the byte
! size is less than 8, each memory byte is automatically filled with
! leading 0s to make an 8-bit byte before these are combined with
! 8-bit bytes from the accumulator."  Note (1) of Table B.5, one page
! earlier in this appendix, uses exactly that ("Specifying a byte size
! of 1 causes each 1 to be expanded to an 8-bit byte 0000 0001").  And
! Reference Manual printed p.75 (PDF 78) is explicit about the
! one-byte case: "if the field length is a multiple of the byte size,
! and if the byte size is less than eight, high-order zeros are added
! to the high-order byte as well as to all other bytes".
!
! So each CONNECT 0011 replaces EIGHT accumulator bits with 0000000b:
! the column bit at the offset, and seven zeros to its left.  The
! offset runs from 63 down to 0, i.e. left to right, so the seven
! zeros of each step land on the seven bits placed just before it.
! Worked through: the bit placed at offset k is erased by the steps at
! offsets k-1 ... k-7, whenever there is one.  Only offset 0 has none.
! EVERY WORD STORED HOLDS ONE BIT, THE LAST OF ITS 64, IN ITS LAST
! POSITION, and 63 zeros.
!
! With "(VBU, 1, 8)" -- a one-bit field in a byte size of eight, which
! is not a whole byte, so "only those bits ... that are included in the
! field length take part in the processing.  No high-order zeros are
! added" (same page) -- one accumulator bit is replaced per step and
! the program transposes the matrix.  That one change is the whole
! correction.  (Table B.7 has byte size 1 too, but with connective
! 0111, OR, where the added zeros are harmless.)
!
! THE MODEL AGREES WITH THE MANUAL ON BOTH: this deck, as printed,
! leaves one bit per word, and the corrected line gives the transpose.
! Nothing is wrong in the model or the assembler here.
!
! THE CHECK IS tests/verify_buchholz_b6.py, which compares EVERY word:
!
!   verify_buchholz_b6.py printed     this deck as it stands.  For row j
!       of the output and word w of that row, the word must be
!       A[64w+63][j] in bit 63 and nothing else.
!   verify_buchholz_b6.py corrected   this deck with line 101.32's
!       (BU,1,1) rewritten as (BU,1,8) by the verifier, in memory.  Row
!       j, word w, bit i of the output must be A[64w+i][j]: the
!       transpose.
!
!   Both also check that A is untouched (256 words), the four guard
!   words, the three index registers and the halt.  The expectations
!   are computed from the A that is in this deck, by the definition of
!   a transpose; nothing is taken from a run.
!
! THE DATA IS THE DECK'S.  n = 2: a 128-by-128 matrix, A.0 = 1000.0
! (256 words, two per row), B.0 = 2000.0.  Bit (r, c) is 1 when
!
!     (r*r + 5*c + r*c + (c >> 3) + 3*(r >> 4) + ((r*c) >> 5)) mod 7 < 3
!
! which gives 128 different rows, 128 different columns, and 7928 of
! the 16384 positions differing from their mirror image.  n = 2 rather
! than 1 so that N.0, 64*N, N and N*N*64.0 are all different numbers.
!
! THE INDEX WORDS, BY HAND:
!   x1  each column: 128 steps of +2.0, then -255.63: a net +0.01.
!       After 128 columns, 1000.0 + 128 bits = 1002.0; count 128 run
!       down to 0; refill 201 = (16)00FA8000000000C9.
!   x2  256 stores of +1.0: 2256.0; count reset to 2 by the last LCI;
!       refill 202 = (16)02340000000800CA.
!   x3  refilled from 203.0 every 64 steps: 0.0, 64, 203
!       = (16)00000000010000CB.
!   PC  the BEW at 106.32, half-word 213.
!
! INJECTIONS, DATA SIDE, OBSERVED through the verifier's second
! argument (a changed copy of this deck), against "corrected" (clean:
! 10 of 10 checks, 512 of 512 words):
!
!   VALUE at 204.0 changed from -255.63 to -255.62, so each column step
!   is two bits:  FAILS 2 of 10 -- x1 (1004.0) and the transpose, 254
!   of its 256 words wrong (row 0 is still column 0; row 1 holds
!   column 2).  A is untouched.
!
!   The count in the index word at 203.0 changed from 64 to 63, so a
!   word is stored after 63 bits:  FAILS 3 of 10 -- x1, x3 and the
!   transpose, all 256 words wrong (the first differs in its last bit
!   only: (16)...4080 for (16)...4081).
!
!   And against "printed", the same VALUE injection (-255.62):  FAILS
!   2 of 10 -- x1 and the one-bit-per-word check, 118 of 256 words
!   wrong (the surviving bit now comes from every other column).
!
! (A change to the matrix itself cannot fail this check, and is not an
! injection: the expectation is computed from the matrix in the deck.)

        SLC, 100.0
START   LX, $1, 201.0                               ! Start
        LX, $2, 202.0
        LX, $3, 203.0
        C0011(V+I)(BU,1,1), 2.0($1), 63($3)         ! (1)   N.0 (X1), 63 (X3)
        V-ICR, $3, 0.32                             ! (2)
        BZXCZ, 101.32                               ! (3)
        ST(V+IC)(BU,64,8), 1.0($2)                  ! (4)
        BZXCZ, 101.32                               ! (5)
        LCI, $2, 2                                  ! (6)   N
        V+C, $1, 204.0                              ! (7)
        BZXCZ, 101.32
        BEW, 106.32                                 ! Stop

        SLC, 201.0
        XW, 1000.0, 128, 201.0                      ! INDEX, A.0, 64*N, 201.0
        XW, 2000.0, 2, 202.0                        ! INDEX, B.0, N, 202.0
        XW, 0.0, 64, 203.0                          ! INDEX, 0.0, 64, 203.0
        VF, -255.63                                 ! VALUE, -N*N*64.0 + 0.01

        SLC, 999.0
        DD(BU,64,8), 99                             ! guard below A
! A: 128 rows of two words, row by row from 1000.0.
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)933226644CC99993      ! row 0, columns 0-63
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)3226644CC9999332      ! row 0, columns 64-127
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)C3C3C3C3E1E1E1E1      ! row 1, columns 0-63
